 # Densification Strategy
-Also called: Adaptive Density Control, Density Control, Densification and Splitting
+Also called: Adaptive Density Control, Densification and Splitting, Primitive Management
 
 ## Overview
-Densification Strategy is the family of training-time optimization mechanisms in 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) that dynamically adjusts the number, distribution, and parameters of Gaussian primitives to balance reconstruction fidelity against computational cost [S1][S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of static representation capacity, where a fixed set of Gaussians cannot capture high-frequency details (under-reconstruction) or becomes redundant in smooth regions (over-reconstruction) [S1][S4, unofficial]. What sets this family apart from static initialization methods is its continuous, iterative process driven by optimization metrics (gradient magnitude, opacity, scale) to spawn, split, or remove primitives, distinguishing it from sibling methods that may use score-based budgets [4705-score-based-densification.md], visibility consistency [10074-gaussian-insertion-and-pruning.md], or error-guided sampling [15896-error-guided-guided-sampling.md].
+Densification strategies are training-time optimization mechanisms for 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) that dynamically adjust the number, distribution, and parameters of Gaussian primitives to balance reconstruction fidelity against computational cost [Parent]. They solve the problem of under-representation (blurry or missing details) and over-representation (redundant primitives causing slow rendering) by periodically spawning, splitting, or removing Gaussians based on optimization metrics [13466-densification-and-splitting.md]. What sets this family apart from static initialization methods is its continuous, iterative nature driven by gradient magnitude, opacity, and scale heuristics, distinguishing it from sibling methods that may use score-based budgets [4705-score-based-densification.md], visibility consistency [10074-gaussian-insertion-and-pruning.md], or error-guided sampling [15896-error-guided-guided-sampling.md].
 
