 ### [Pruning and Sparsification](12354-pruning-and-sparsification.md)
-Pruning and Sparsification refers to the family of strategies that reduce the cardinality of the Gaussian set $\mathcal{G}$ by removing redundant or insignificant primitives, thereby lowering memory usage and rendering cost without degrading visual fidelity. It solves the problem of unbounded Gaussian growth and the persistence of "floaters" or boundary artifacts that occur when standard densification creates excessive redundancy. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Adaptive Density Control](9188-adaptive-density-control.md) by focusing specifically on the removal phase (or combined removal/addition balance) rather than the generative splitting/cloning logic, and differs from [Principled Uncertainty Pruning](9680-principled-uncertainty-pruning.md) by often relying on simpler opacity or significance thresholds rather than second-order Fisher information matrices.
+Pruning and Sparsification are training-time strategies for 3D Gaussian Splatting that control the population of primitives $\mathcal{G}$ to balance representation density against overfitting and computational cost. It solves the problem of unbounded Gaussian growth and the persistence of "floaters" or boundary artifacts by periodically evaluating metrics (opacity, gradient, scale) to remove redundant primitives or reinitialize them. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Significance-Aware Pruning](9676-significance-aware-pruning.md) and [Principled Uncertainty Pruning](9680-principled-uncertainty-pruning.md) by focusing on opacity thresholds, transmittance consistency during cloning, and fixed-schedule resets rather than adaptive uncertainty bounds or significance scores derived from view counts.
 
